    "url": "https://repbashline.com/News/38752/Press-Releases/Bashline--Pittman--Hutchinson-Secure-Over--2-3-Million-in-State-Funding-for-Clarion--Armstrong-Counties",
    "date": null,
    "raw_text": "Bashline, Pittman, Hutchinson Secure Over $2.3 Million in State Funding for Clarion, Armstrong Counties\nSeptember 15, 2026\nHARRISBURG – Rep. Josh Bashline (R-63) and Sens. Joe Pittman (R-41) and Scott Hutchinson (R-21) secured over $2.3 million in Local Share Account (LSA) grant funding for Clarion and Armstrong counties to support various local infrastructure, public safety and community resource projects.\nThe following allocations were obtained by Bashline and Pittman:\n•\nCowanshannock Township Municipal Authority: $1 million\nto repair and replace water treatment valves and filters.\n•\nWayne Township: $426,835\nto purchase a road grader.\n•\nWorthington West Franklin Joint Municipal Authority: $162,000\nto clean water wells in West Franklin Township.\nBashline and Hutchinson acquired funding for the following projects:\n•\nClarion County: $271,872\nto purchase and upfit four police vehicles for Clarion County Sheriff’s Department.\n•\nLimestone Township: $157,000\nto buy a tractor.\n•\nClarion Borough: $122,000\nto acquire an upfitted plow truck.\n•\nEast Brady Borough: $103,000\nto buy a truck upfitted with a snowplow, a stainless-steel gas spreader, and an LED light bar; and a roller to be used to compact bituminous materials during road patching and paving work.\n•\nClarion Borough: $58,000\nto purchase a police vehicle with an upfit package for the Clarion Borough Police Department.\n“This announcement represents a commitment to our communities and the people who call them home,” said Bashline. “By securing these critical resources, we can address pressing needs, strengthen our neighborhoods and improve services. It is a privilege to fight for valuable funding in Harrisburg, and it would not be possible without Sens. Pittman and Hutchinson.”\n“Projects to strengthen communities are critical to the stability and growth of our region,” Pittman said. “I was pleased to work with my colleagues in Harrisburg to advocate for this substantial support. Thoughtful investments in local infrastructure and economic development initiatives help ensure municipalities can meet the needs of residents now and into the future. This is an exciting day, as the funding will help address local priorities, support economic growth, and improve quality of life across the county.”\nThe grants were approved and awarded by the Commonwealth Financing Authority, which was established as an independent agency that holds fiduciary responsibility over the funding of programs and investments in Pennsylvania’s economic growth. LSA grants are derived from gaming revenues.\nRepresentative Josh Bashline\n63rd Legislative District\nPennsylvania House of Representatives\nMedia Contact: Nate Temple\n717-260-6396\nntemple@pahousegop.com\nhttps://www.repbashline.com/ / https://www.facebook.com/repbashline\nShare\nSign Up to Receive Legislative Email Updates\nKeep up-to-date on the latest legislative and community news. Your email address will be used strictly for legislative purposes.\nEmail*\nZIP Code\nSubscribe",
    "ai_summary": {
      "summary": "State Representatives and Senators from the Clarion and Armstrong County area announced $2.3 million in Local Share Account grants approved by the Commonwealth Financing Authority. The grants, funded by gaming revenues, will pay for water infrastructure repairs, road equipment purchases, and police vehicles across several townships and boroughs. The funding was not directly secured by the legislators but allocated through an independent state agency.",
