  {
    "title": "Bashline, Rapp, Smith Demand Immediate Action to Avert Full Closure of Local Tourist Attraction",
    "url": "https://repbashline.com/News/37944/Press-Releases/Bashline--Rapp--Smith-Demand-Immediate-Action-to-Avert-Full-Closure-of-Local-Tourist-Attraction",
    "date": null,
    "raw_text": "Bashline, Rapp, Smith Demand Immediate Action to Avert Full Closure of Local Tourist Attraction\nMay 6, 2026\nHARRISBURG – The Pennsylvania Department of Conservation and Natural Resources (DCNR) is planning to close Ridge Campground in Cook Forest State Park, an 8,500-acre park that runs through Clarion, Forest and Jefferson counties, for the entire 2027 season to build three new shower houses and tear down four existing shower houses. Ridge Campground, one of the largest in Pennsylvania's state park system, contains approximately 210 campsites and serves thousands of families each year.\nReps. Josh Bashline (R-Clarion/Armstrong), Kathy Rapp (R- Crawford/Forest/Warren) and Brian Smith (R- Jefferson/Indiana) are calling on DCNR to immediately intervene and direct the appropriate agencies to review the plan due to the anticipated negative, long-term economic impacts. According to the members, 30 area businesses depend on tourism for survival.\n“For countless families, Ridge Campground and Cook Forest State Park are where memories are made year after year,” said Bashline. “It is a cornerstone of northwest Pennsylvania, and we cannot stand idly by while the local economy hangs in the balance. Keeping it open must occur for everyone’s benefit.”\n“Closing Ridge Campground for the full season is unnecessary and shortsighted,” Rapp said. “The community deserves a solution that will facilitate these improvements without undermining local livelihoods.”\n“Closing the campground for the entire summer will severely impact the local businesses that depend on seasonal visitors,” Smith said. “The long-term economic harm that may be done easily outweighs the minor benefits of a few new shower houses. The current plan is not in the community’s best interest.”\nIn addition to the economic harm, this closure displaces thousands of families who have long-standing traditions at Cook Forest. If residents are forced to find alternatives, the members express concern about the possibility of visitors not returning.\nIn their letter to DCNR, Bashline, Rapp and Smith suggest the best solution would be to phase the improvements in so portions of the campground stay open throughout the entire season. By taking on their plan, prospective visitors would know about ongoing construction and how to best navigate it.\nEditor’s Note:\nHere is the letter sent by the members to the DCNR\n.\nRepresentative Josh Bashline\n63rd Legislative District\nPennsylvania House of Representatives\nMedia Contact: Nate Temple\n717-260-6396\nntemple@pahousegop.com\nhttps://www.repbashline.com/ / https://www.facebook.com/repbashline\nShare\nSign Up to Receive Legislative Email Updates\nKeep up-to-date on the latest legislative and community news. Your email address will be used strictly for legislative purposes.\nEmail*\nZIP Code\nSubscribe",
    "ai_summary": {
      "summary": "Three Republican state representatives sent a letter to the Pennsylvania Department of Conservation and Natural Resources opposing its plan to fully close Ridge Campground at Cook Forest State Park for the entire 2027 season during shower house construction. They are asking DCNR to review the plan and instead phase construction so parts of the campground remain open. No legislation was introduced.",
      "action_type": "statement",
      "topics": [
        "economy",
        "environment",
        "other"
      ],
      "spin_flag": true
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "Bashline Secures Major Investment for Local Water and Sewer Authority",
    "url": "https://repbashline.com/News/37841/Press-Releases/Bashline-Secures-Major-Investment-for-Local-Water-and-Sewer-Authority",
    "date": null,
    "raw_text": "Bashline Secures Major Investment for Local Water and Sewer Authority\nApril 15, 2026\nHARRISBURG – Rep. Josh Bashline (R-Clarion/Armstrong) announced his efforts were successful in obtaining $5.176 million in grant and loan funding from the Pennsylvania Infrastructure Investment Authority (PENNVEST) to help the Foxburg Area Water and Sewer Authority with several extensive undertakings. Out of the funding allocation, more than $2.6 million is deemed to be a grant.\nAccording to Bashline, a new water treatment plant will be built next to the current facility and will include four greensand filters, chemical feed systems and other necessary equipment. The existing water storage tank, which is too small to handle current needs, will be replaced with a new 150,000-gallon tank.\nIn addition, nearly the entire water distribution system will be replaced, which includes about 6,500 feet of six-inch waterline, 300 feet of four-inch waterline and 700 feet of two-inch waterline. Several new fire hydrants will also be installed throughout the system.\nTo ensure reliable service during power outages, an emergency generator will also be added to the new treatment plant.\n“Our local officials saw the need for immediate action and stepped up to the plate to collaborate with me in securing the funds,” said Bashline. “The investment of more than $5 million will have a lasting impact on the community as it will help modernize infrastructure, improve service and help protect the long-term health and safety of residents.”\nFounded in 1988, PENNVEST provides grants and low-interest loans for design, engineering and construction of publicly and privately owned drinking water distribution and treatment facilities, as well as storm water and wastewater projects. For more information on the PENNVEST program, visit\npennvest.pa.gov\n.\nRepresentative Josh Bashline\n63rd Legislative District\nPennsylvania House of Representatives\nMedia Contact: Nate Temple\n717-260-6396\nntemple@pahousegop.com\nhttps://www.repbashline.com/ / https://www.facebook.com/repbashline\nShare\nSign Up to Receive Legislative Email Updates\nKeep up-to-date on the latest legislative and community news. Your email address will be used strictly for legislative purposes.\nEmail*\nZIP Code\nSubscribe",
    "ai_summary": {
      "summary": "The Foxburg Area Water and Sewer Authority received $5.176 million in PENNVEST funding, including over $2.6 million in grants, for water infrastructure upgrades. The project will include a new water treatment plant, a larger storage tank, replacement of most of the water distribution system, new fire hydrants, and an emergency generator. Rep. Bashline assisted in the funding application process.",
      "action_type": "budget",
      "topics": [
        "infrastructure",
        "public_safety"
      ],
      "spin_flag": true
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "Bashline to Host Concealed Carry Seminar April 30",
    "url": "https://repbashline.com/News/37804/Press-Releases/Bashline-to-Host-Concealed-Carry-Seminar-April-30",
    "date": null,
    "raw_text": "Bashline to Host Concealed Carry Seminar April 30\nApril 7, 2026\nHARRISBURG –Rep. Josh Bashline (R-Clarion/Armstrong) announced he will be hosting his annual Concealed Carry Seminar on Thursday, April 30, from 5-7 p.m., at the New Bethlehem Fire Company No. 1, 425 Arch St., New Bethlehem.\n“I am pleased to host this event once again to give law-abiding citizens pertinent information on how they can legally protect themselves, their families and their properties,” said Bashline. “This is a great interactive forum where attendees can ask questions and gain deeper insight on the information they need when it comes to owning a firearm.”\nVarious representatives will be on hand to discuss a wide range of firearms safety and Second Amendment topics. This includes how to obtain a permit to carry a concealed weapon, what to do when encountering a member of law enforcement while carrying a concealed weapon, details surrounding Pennsylvania’s Castle Doctrine and other valuable information regarding state gun laws.\nRegistration is required. Constituents may call the Clarion District office at 814-226-9000 or visit\nRepBashline.com/events\n.\nRepresentative Josh Bashline\n63rd Legislative District\nPennsylvania House of Representatives\nMedia Contact: Nate Temple\n717-260-6396\nntemple@pahousegop.com\nhttps://www.repbashline.com/\nhttps://www.facebook.com/repbashline\nShare\nSign Up to Receive Legislative Email Updates\nKeep up-to-date on the latest legislative and community news. Your email address will be used strictly for legislative purposes.\nEmail*\nZIP Code\nSubscribe",
    "ai_summary": {
      "summary": "State Representative Josh Bashline is hosting a public seminar on April 30 in New Bethlehem covering Pennsylvania concealed carry permits, gun laws, the Castle Doctrine, and interactions with law enforcement while armed. The event runs from 5–7 p.m. at the New Bethlehem Fire Company and requires advance registration. Various speakers will present and take questions from attendees.",
