  {
    "title": "Fiscal Stewardship, Fair Funding Critical for Next State Budget, Says Bashline",
    "url": "https://repbashline.com/News/37464/Press-Releases/Fiscal-Stewardship--Fair-Funding-Critical-for-Next-State-Budget--Says-Bashline",
    "date": null,
    "raw_text": "Fiscal Stewardship, Fair Funding Critical for Next State Budget, Says Bashline\nFebruary 4, 2026\nHARRISBURG – Tuesday, Gov. Josh Shapiro delivered his state budget address to outline his $53.26 billion proposal for Fiscal Year 2026-27 – a 5.4% increase in spending over the current fiscal year. Rep. Josh Bashline (R-Clarion/Armstrong) released the following statement, urging fiscal discipline and fair funding for the 63rd District:\n“Just how it was last year, the governor’s proposal is not based in reality. It spends well past our means and will take $4.58 billion out of the Rainy Day Fund, a resource House Republicans fought so hard to build up in case of emergencies.\n“He is also calling for new taxes and changes to the current tax structure totaling $1.88 billion in new revenue, primarily generated through taxing skill games and recreational marijuana, while also calling for a minimum wage increase to $15 an hour for non-tipped workers and $9 per hour for tipped workers.\n“Exercising discipline ensures taxpayers will keep more of their hard-earned dollars at a time when prices are running out of control. As families feel the tensions from the tightening of financial belts, they deserve necessary relief to ensure they don’t have to pick and choose which necessities they can pay for.\n“In the district I’m so honored to serve, we love and appreciate our communities. During my short time, the 63rd District has not received our fair share of taxpayer funds to improve our communities. Our tax dollars need to stay in Clarion and Armstrong counties so we can get a good return on our investments for townships, first responders, schools and more.\n“If the budget does not satisfy these requirements, I’ll be a definitive ‘no’ on passage. Budget negotiations will begin soon, and I hope my feedback is included in the final product.”\nRepresentative Josh Bashline\n63rd Legislative District\nPennsylvania House of Representatives\nMedia Contact: Nate Temple\n717-260-6396\nntemple@pahousegop.com\nhttps://www.repbashline.com/\nhttps://www.facebook.com/repbashline\nShare\nSign Up to Receive Legislative Email Updates\nKeep up-to-date on the latest legislative and community news. Your email address will be used strictly for legislative purposes.\nEmail*\nZIP Code\nSubscribe",
    "ai_summary": {
      "summary": "Rep. Josh Bashline issued a statement responding to Governor Shapiro's proposed $53.26 billion state budget for FY 2026-27. Bashline criticized the proposal as fiscally irresponsible, objecting to its use of Rainy Day Fund reserves and proposed tax increases. He stated he will vote against the budget unless it includes more funding directed to his district and reflects greater spending restraint.",
      "action_type": "statement",
      "topics": [
        "economy",
        "education",
        "other"
      ],
      "spin_flag": true
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "Bashline Secures Significant Funding for Redbank Valley Municipal Authority",
    "url": "https://repbashline.com/News/37340/Press-Releases/Bashline-Secures-Significant-Funding-for-Redbank-Valley-Municipal-Authority",
    "date": null,
    "raw_text": "Bashline Secures Significant Funding for Redbank Valley Municipal Authority\nJanuary 21, 2026\nHARRISBURG – Rep. Josh Bashline (R-Clarion/Armstrong) announced he secured more than $7.3 million in grant and loan funding from the Pennsylvania Infrastructure Investment Authority (PENNVEST) to assist the Redbank Valley Municipal Authority in Clarion County with a water quality project. Out of this allocation, $5.385 million is deemed a grant.\nThe project will include installing nearly 9,880 feet of six-inch waterline, 2,700 feet of eight-inch waterline and 1,800 feet of 10-inch waterline. Several fire hydrants, valves and other appurtenancesare incorporated. As for the water treatment plant, new inclined plane settlers will be installed to restore aging tube settlers while filter media will also be replaced.\n“These projects play a significant role in improving the quality of life in the places we call home,” said Bashline. “I am grateful for the swift work by the leadership at Redbank Valley Municipal Authority to bring this to my attention, where I then successfully advocated for this significant award. I am proud of the efforts that led to this result.”\nFounded in 1988, PENNVEST provides grants and low-interest loans for design, engineering and construction of publicly and privately owned drinking water distribution and treatment facilities, as well as storm water and wastewater projects. For more information on the PENNVEST program, visit\npennvest.pa.gov\n.\nRepresentative Josh Bashline\n63rd Legislative District\nPennsylvania House of Representatives\nMedia Contact: Nate Temple\n717-260-6396\nntemple@pahousegop.com\nhttps://www.repbashline.com/ / https://www.facebook.com/repbashline\nShare\nSign Up to Receive Legislative Email Updates\nKeep up-to-date on the latest legislative and community news. Your email address will be used strictly for legislative purposes.\nEmail*\nZIP Code\nSubscribe",
    "ai_summary": {
      "summary": "The Redbank Valley Municipal Authority in Clarion County received $7.3 million in PENNVEST funding ($5.385 million of which is a grant) for water infrastructure upgrades, including new waterline installations and water treatment plant improvements. Rep. Bashline announced the funding award. PENNVEST is a state authority that distributes grants and loans for water infrastructure projects.",
      "action_type": "other",
      "topics": [
        "infrastructure",
        "environment"
      ],
      "spin_flag": true
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "More Than $1.17 Million in State Funding Allocated to District 63, Says Bashline",
    "url": "https://repbashline.com/News/37282/Press-Releases/More-Than--1-17-Million-in-State-Funding-Allocated-to-District-63--Says-Bashline",
    "date": null,
    "raw_text": "More Than $1.17 Million in State Funding Allocated to District 63, Says Bashline\nJanuary 20, 2026\nHARRISBURG –Rep. Josh Bashline (R-Clarion/Armstrong) announced more than $1.17 million in state funding is heading to District 63 to assist with three local improvement projects.\n“True progress is measured not by statistics alone, but by how effectively our actions improve the everyday quality of life for the people we serve,” said Bashline. “This news aligns with this mission, and our district will benefit in the long term.”\nAshland Township is obtaining more than $676,600 from the Department of Community and Economic Development’s (DCED) Multimodal Transportation Fund (MTF) for roadway improvements on Camp Coffman Road. Just over $279,000 from the MTF is being allocated Armstrong County to repave roads throughout Ford City, Manorville Borough, Hovey Township, Sugarcreek Township, Kittanning Township and West Kittanning Township.\nEastern Armstrong County Municipal Authority is receiving just over $219,000 from the Commonwealth Financing Authority’s (CFA) Pennsylvania Small Water and Sewer Program, to replace aging pumps at the treatment plant on Old Elderton Hill Road and at the pump station on Westview Terrace Road. A waterline at the storage tank on Shadyside Farm Drive will be relocated, eliminating the need for an air release valve that is prone to failure. Additionally, this project will require the addition of five fire hydrants and five valves.\nThe Pennsylvania Small Water and Sewer Program awards grants to municipalities and municipal authorities for water, sewer, storm water and flood control projects with a cost of not less than $30,000 and not more than $500,000. Funds may be used toward construction, improvement, expansion or rehabilitation or repair of a water supply system, sanitary sewer system, storm sewer system or flood control projects.\nThe MTF provides grants to encourage economic development and ensure that a safe and reliable system of transportation is available to the residents of the Commonwealth. Funds may be used for the development, rehabilitation and enhancement of transportation assets to existing communities, streetscape, lighting, sidewalk enhancement, pedestrian safety, connectivity of transportation assets and transit-oriented development.\nRepresentative Josh Bashline\n63rd Legislative District\nPennsylvania House of Representatives\nMedia Contact: Nate Temple\n717-260-6396\nntemple@pahousegop.com\nhttps://www.repbashline.com/\nhttps://www.facebook.com/repbashline\nShare\nSign Up to Receive Legislative Email Updates\nKeep up-to-date on the latest legislative and community news. Your email address will be used strictly for legislative purposes.\nEmail*\nZIP Code\nSubscribe",
    "ai_summary": {
      "summary": "State agencies have allocated approximately $1.17 million in grants to three projects in Pennsylvania's 63rd legislative district: over $676,600 for road improvements on Camp Coffman Road in Ashland Township, about $279,000 to repave roads across multiple Armstrong County municipalities, and roughly $219,000 for water and sewer infrastructure upgrades by the Eastern Armstrong County Municipal Authority. Rep. Bashline announced the funding allocations, which were awarded through existing state grant programs administered by the Department of Community and Economic Development and the Commonwealth Financing Authority.",
      "action_type": "statement",
      "topics": [
        "infrastructure",
        "economy"
      ],
      "spin_flag": true
    }
  },
