  {
    "title": "Defending Our Daughters: Make the Promise",
    "url": "https://repbashline.com/News/37226/Press-Releases/Defending-Our-Daughters--Make-the-Promise",
    "date": null,
    "raw_text": "Defending Our Daughters: Make the Promise\nJanuary 7, 2026\nHARRISBURG – Continuing his advocacy for fairness in women’s sports, Rep. Josh Bashline (R-Clarion/Armstrong) is inviting people to join the “Defending Our Daughters” campaign to show support for the Commonwealth’s female athletes.\nAll Pennsylvanians are invited to sign the “I Believe” promise to highlight the rights of girls to have a safe, fair and equal playing field; to compete to win games, championships and titles; to earn scholarships; and to have private locker rooms spaces, without males being present.\nThe promise also reinforces the shared responsibility of all Pennsylvanians, and specifically the state’s leaders, to get off the sidelines to protect and defend female athletes.\n“Female athletes deserve to feel safe and have fair opportunities in athletic competitions,” said Bashline. “Title IX was a massive victory for our country. Respecting female athletes means defending the principles that uphold a level playing field and let hard work, talent and dedication determine outcomes. Now is the time to act, and I hope residents in Clarion and Armstrong counties will join me in this fight.”\nThe “I Believe” promise is available online for people to sign at\ndefendingdaughters.com\n. Hard copies are also available in his Clarion district office, located at 160 South 2nd Ave., Suite C, as well as his Kittanning district office at 200 N. Jefferson St. The information will be shared with Gov. Josh Shapiro and House leaders to support action on the issue this year.\nSo far this session, majority House Democrats have avoided votes on three different bills aimed at protecting women’s sports.\nSenate Bill 9\n, which passed the Senate with bipartisan support in May, was initially – and appropriately – referred to the House Education Committee. To avoid a discharge resolution, the bill was re-referred to the Health Committee and is now in the Judiciary Committee.\nHouse Bills 158\nand\n1849\nwere also initially referred to the Education Committee but were re-referred to Judiciary in December, also to avoid discharge resolutions that would force a vote on the bills.\nRepresentative Josh Bashline\n63rd Legislative District\nPennsylvania House of Representatives\nMedia Contact: Nate Temple\n717-260-6396\nntemple@pahousegop.com\nhttps://www.repbashline.com/\nhttps://www.facebook.com/repbashline\nShare\nSign Up to Receive Legislative Email Updates\nKeep up-to-date on the latest legislative and community news. Your email address will be used strictly for legislative purposes.\nEmail*\nZIP Code\nSubscribe",
    "ai_summary": {
      "summary": "Rep. Josh Bashline is promoting an online and in-person petition campaign called 'Defending Our Daughters' that asks Pennsylvanians to sign a pledge supporting restrictions on transgender athletes in women's sports. The petition signatures are intended to be delivered to the governor and House leadership to pressure action on related legislation. He also notes that three bills on the topic — Senate Bill 9, House Bills 158 and 1849 — have not received floor votes in the House this session.",
      "action_type": "statement",
      "topics": [
        "education",
        "public_safety",
        "other"
      ],
      "spin_flag": true
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "Bashline: Nearly $646,000 in State Funding Dedicated to 63rd District",
    "url": "https://repbashline.com/News/37131/Press-Releases/Bashline--Nearly--646-000-in-State-Funding-Dedicated-to-63rd-District",
    "date": null,
    "raw_text": "Bashline: Nearly $646,000 in State Funding Dedicated to 63rd District\nDecember 18, 2025\nHARRISBURG\n– Rep. Josh Bashline (R-Clarion/Armstrong) announced today nearly $646,000 in Local Share Account (LSA) grant funding is heading to the 63rd District to support various local infrastructure, public safety and community resource projects.\nThe following projects were approved for funding:\n•\nWorthington West Franklin Joint Municipal Authority: $147,400 to install water treatment plant communication upgrades at the facilities located in West Franklin Township.\n•\nRedbank Township: $118,075 to buy a tractor with a bucket to be used in the township.\n•\nHawthorn Borough: $100,225 to acquire radios and skid steer attachment equipment, as well as a tractor with attachments.\n•\nAshland Township: $98,870 to purchase a teleskid for use in the township.\n•\nClarion Borough: $70,178 to buy a fire rescue utility vehicle for Clarion Fire and Hose Company No. 1.\n•\nBeaver Township: $66,057 to purchase a new tractor and boom mower attachment for the township.\n•\nUnion Area Council of Governments (COG): $45,000 to complete a feasibility study for the Union COG Pool Park located in Sligo Borough, Clarion County.\n“This is a massive win for the district I am grateful to represent,” said Bashline. “This was a team effort where I collaborated with each organization’s officials, as well as Sens. Scott Hutchinson and Joe Pittman. I am proud to see our hard work pay off so we can improve the lives of residents in Clarion and Armstrong counties.”\nThe grants were approved and awarded by the Commonwealth Financing Authority, which was established as an independent agency that holds fiduciary responsibility over the funding of programs and investments in Pennsylvania’s economic growth. LSA grants are derived from gaming revenues.\nRepresentative Josh Bashline\n63rd Legislative District\nPennsylvania House of Representatives\nMedia Contact: Nate Temple\n717-260-6396\nntemple@pahousegop.com\nShare\nSign Up to Receive Legislative Email Updates\nKeep up-to-date on the latest legislative and community news. Your email address will be used strictly for legislative purposes.\nEmail*\nZIP Code\nSubscribe",
    "ai_summary": {
      "summary": "Rep. Josh Bashline announced that seven municipalities and authorities in his district received a combined $645,805 in state gaming revenue grants through the Local Share Account program. The funds, approved by the Commonwealth Financing Authority, will go toward equipment purchases (tractors, vehicles, radios) and infrastructure upgrades across Clarion and Armstrong counties. Bashline's role was to work with local officials and state senators to facilitate the grant applications.",
      "action_type": "other",
      "topics": [
        "infrastructure",
        "public_safety",
        "economy"
      ],
      "spin_flag": true
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "More Than $1.35 Million Dedicated to District 63 for Revitalization Efforts, Says Bashline",
    "url": "https://repbashline.com/News/37087/Press-Releases/More-Than--1-35-Million-Dedicated-to-District-63-for-Revitalization-Efforts--Says-Bashline",
    "date": null,
    "raw_text": "More Than $1.35 Million Dedicated to District 63 for Revitalization Efforts, Says Bashline\nDecember 17, 2025\nHARRISBURG – Rep. Josh Bashline (R-Clarion/Armstrong) announced today more than $1.35 million in funding, through the Department of Community and Economic Development’s (DCED) Neighborhood Assistance Program (NAP), is heading to the 63rd District.\nThe NAP offers tax credits to businesses supporting nonprofit community revitalization efforts, benefiting 405 projects statewide. There are five main components: Charitable Food Program (CFP), Neighborhood Assistance Program (NAP), Special Program Priorities (SPP), the Neighborhood Partnership Program (NPP), and the Enterprise Zone Program (EZP).\nBelow are the descriptions of entities and missions:\n•\nClarion County Adventures: $715,950 (SPP)\nto complete construction of a year-round ADA-accessible icehouse in Clarion. The facility will transform a blighted property into a vibrant public park, providing inclusive recreation for low-income residents and serving an estimated 5,265 people.\n•\nArmstrong Habitat for Humanity: $418,000 (NPP)\nto revitalize Kittanning Borough through a six-year plan. The project will address blight, improve housing, and upgrade infrastructure through affordable home construction and home repairs, trail resurfacing and public art.\n•\nWolf’s Corners Fair Association: $225,126 (SPP)\nto build the Youth and Agricultural Building, playground and stormwater infrastructure, which will transform the fairgrounds into a year-round hub, providing safe, modern facilities and recreation for over 9,500 rural residents.\n“These organizations are pivotal in addressing community needs in an effective, timely manner,” said Bashline. “I cannot thank them enough for all the great work they do, and I hope the awards help these entities continue to provide meaningful support to Clarion and Armstrong counties.”\nThe SPP targets specific problems and projects which the state has designated as priorities in a distressed area. A business firm contributing to an approved SPP application would be eligible for up to a 90% tax credit.\nThe NPP uses long-term collaborations of five years or more involving business, government and community leaders to produce a comprehensive, asset-based and relationship-driven approach to community development. A tax credit of up to 90% or 95% can be awarded based on the length of the program.\nRepresentative Josh Bashline\n63rd Legislative District\nPennsylvania House of Representatives\nMedia Contact: Nate Temple\n717-260-6396\nntemple@pahousegop.com\nhttps://www.repbashline.com/\nhttps://www.facebook.com/repbashline\nShare\nSign Up to Receive Legislative Email Updates\nKeep up-to-date on the latest legislative and community news. Your email address will be used strictly for legislative purposes.\nEmail*\nZIP Code\nSubscribe",
    "ai_summary": {
      "summary": "The Pennsylvania Department of Community and Economic Development awarded over $1.35 million in Neighborhood Assistance Program tax credits to three nonprofit organizations in Rep. Bashline's district. The funds will support an ADA-accessible ice rink in Clarion, affordable housing and blight removal in Kittanning Borough, and agricultural fairground improvements in rural Armstrong County. Bashline announced the awards but did not author the program; he is publicizing state funding that went to organizations in his district.",
      "action_type": "statement",
      "topics": [
        "economy",
        "housing",
        "infrastructure"
      ],
